<!-- THE STORY -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h3>He owns the character. That was the problem.</h3>
    <p>He drew a kids&rsquo; character that tells them to walk away from a fight.</p>
    <p>Grant money exists for exactly this work. Almost none of it will pay a person directly. It pays organizations.</p>
    <p>So he sat on something good with no door into the room where the money is.</p>

    <h3>We built the door.</h3>
    <p>First, a plain budget. What it actually costs to get the character back in front of kids, in schools, at events. Real numbers on a real page.</p>
    <p>Then, the structure question: how to take money in without handing away what he made, and without a loan hanging over him.</p>
    <p>Then, people. A nonprofit board member. A senior loan advisor who now sits in on the planning.</p>

    <h3>Most of the work was getting four calendars to agree.</h3>
    <p>A webinar. An exercise class. A wedding anniversary. Weeks of small emails to put four people on one call.</p>
    <p>That is the unglamorous part, and it is the part that moved it.</p>

    <h3>Where it stands.</h3>
    <p>He walked in with a character. He now has a budget, an advisor, a nonprofit contact, and a scheduled meeting to sign things.</p>
    <p>Same idea. A path underneath it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT THIS PROVES -->
<section class="ink">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What this proves</h2>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">An idea locked inside one person&rsquo;s name can be opened up, with the right structure and the patience to keep calling until the right people are in the same room.</p>
